417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1031 "The Average of the Roots Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1033 1451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5555aaf2-f73f-5c86-8f74-5c76cb5a6f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1031 "The Midpoint Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1033 1387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051064 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7dfa1b95-cfc1-582c-b69d-947aa000c510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110377 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110377 #edb/ref 1071 "Which number corresponds to the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ shown below?\n\n![](images/88/8850f207b51c6789073a0fb83b221a93290ade40b3f148248cac803fc1486452.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f46e0c53-c110-5990-b3ea-5dd27991cdc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110377 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $A$ corresponds to the number $x_1=-1$ while $B$ corresponds to $x_2=5$.\n\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to the midpoint $M$, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1 + 5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=2$.\n\n![](images/07/07d9c940fbe2e5a14c5dc3fc1b46add8947f9ad0df7151f8693793cf70359688.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110378 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110378 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/bf/bf08731ef8b88b55c1b3df41e58443beefb5e1a69b46757fbfb18c22d6c0b6b1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c337d65-54d7-5264-b750-1a7f7bcd07f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110378 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overline{PM}$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2PM &= PQ \\\\\n2(4x - 3) &= 5x + 9 \\\\\n8x - 6 &= 5x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x - 6 - 5x &= 5x + 9 - 5x \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 6 + 6 &= 9 + 6 \\\\\n3x &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110379 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110379 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{LN}$. What is the value of $x$ if $MN=4x+1$ centimeters and $LM=3x+4$ centimeters?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e321a78-087f-5962-a88c-e61bdc10de9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110379 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/60/60e50f27aef61d5a97f9165dab922fa25cd80c114a0b11bbfbcdfe4c0fdf90e9.png)\n\nSince $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{LN}$, the two segments $\\overline{LM}$ and $\\overline{MN}$ have the same length. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLM &= MN \\\\\n3x + 4 &= 4x + 1 \\\\\n3x + 4 - 3x &= 4x + 1 - 3x \\\\\n4 &= x + 1 \\\\\n4 - 1 &= x + 1 - 1 \\\\\n3 &= x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\n." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110380 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110380 #edb/ref 1071 "For the diagram depicted below, which number corresponds to the point $R$ if $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{RS}$?\n\n![](images/d4/d4b49c7832633378a281295850be72f2ff664475b79f9b692591411c2d9ed6c4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c79d8054-b4f5-57e3-b816-5ac07ccfa90d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110380 #edb/ref 1074 "From the picture, we can see that the point $S$ corresponds to the number $x_2=3$ while the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=1$.\n\nTo find the number $x_1$ corresponding to the point $R$, we first set up an equation using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 1 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 - 3 &= x_{1} \\\\\n- 1 &= x_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $R$ corresponds to $x_1=-1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4897cf174e575e4999ba9605c9ddd03b3820c63fd77a1d7f87fdcaac171e35b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110744 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Midpoint Using the Midpoint Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110744 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110744 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1f6f3cc2-e0b5-5263-896d-53369438a2f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110745 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110745 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Properties of Midpoints to Calculate an Unknown Value From a Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110745 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110745 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fb88880e-8b94-5223-b3fb-a4d80e795a00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110746 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Properties of Midpoints to Calculate an Unknown Value From a Description" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110746 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110746 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c4a4cad5-d0b0-51cb-921f-31a96e8e5eda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110747 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110747 #edb/ref 1039 "Using the Midpoint Formula to Calculate an Endpoint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110747 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110747 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c451d750-c40f-5b3f-9cd2-9eec197809de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1045 "Midpoints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "01608397-be37-5684-a088-261c3d612344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1061 10013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f54758a4-a9bd-55d8-8fe5-726424159290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1061 8710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb8d8caf-a880-55f9-8ec4-027667c4d158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1061 8711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "071ff184-b63f-5999-8a03-a464cb40e4a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1061 8712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8ed3410-17c6-5b4e-801f-204a8559cabe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1061 8713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "143b8bf4-dc04-56fe-9f49-a538e4b75644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1061 8714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45c8bbe7-572c-5f45-8330-89214603ae4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1064 "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1065 2165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9e476e67-9dd4-5098-be96-fc805e9e97f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M$, we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{3 + 7}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{2} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5$.\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1065 2347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7b14d3c0-0c84-55bc-be21-3359fbc5f6ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1067 "The Midpoint Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
